From 3e8c45017c943397389c4b848fc7d58cc3e19464 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Codex Executor <codex-executor@local>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 16:28:45 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 14/22] sheet surface: near-plane treatment decided by topology
 and material side

The census of record left three surface failures and their diagnosis
pointed at one mechanism, the blanket "snap every vertex within eps onto
the plane" of the remesh-and-clip stage (and the tangent-patch pass added
on top of it):

- a vertex that merely passes near a face with the void in between was
  pulled onto the face, fabricating a contact where the exact geometry has
  a gap; the cap beside it had no carrier support and stayed open
  (pop_cut_0475);
- snapping the two endpoints of an interior edge while its neighbours stay
  off the plane pinches the sheet onto the cap along that edge, an edge
  with four triangles (pop_cut_0620, and pop_cut_0076 earlier);
- the tangent-patch pass moved vertices up to half a mesh size and folded
  triangles beside a grazing cut (pop_cut_0625).

_snap_crossings_and_flatten_patches replaces both.  A near vertex whose
one-ring reaches clearly beyond the plane on both sides is a crossing and
is moved onto the plane (jointly with the planes it already lies on,
displacement capped).  Any other near vertex belongs to a patch running
alongside the plane; patches are handled by connected component: with the
material between patch and plane the whole patch is moved onto the plane
and the flat-triangle drop turns the slab into cap, with the void between
nothing moves.  _flatten_tangent_edges enforces the invariant behind this
("no interior sheet edge in a cap plane with material below") after every
step that moves or merges vertices, flattening the two triangles of such
an edge unless the move would fold a neighbour; the guard is the fold
itself, not a distance threshold.

The three residual cells and the four earlier ones build watertight
surfaces; the 20-cell regression panel is unchanged in status with
triangle counts within 1 percent.
